From 73a82adec4a41c3f53f45c4b7c8be21f8f30023f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:55:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Batches: memory spaces, execution policies and memory
 resources

- The location of the arrays is checked against the memory space exported
  by the library (`<f>_generic_parallel_memory_space`: host, device or
  any), instead of a list of backends written in MGIS.
- The functions of a point (integrate, initialize functions,
  post-processings, rotations, C bindings) report an error for a
  behaviour generated by the generic-parallel interface, instead of
  calling a null pointer.
- The values held by MGIS (uniform values, LOCAL_STORAGE) are refused
  when the memory of the host is not accessible by the computations.
- The mass density is only mapped for the speed of sound, and may be
  given by a span on one value. A uniform material property stored
  locally is stored as a scalar.
- Initialize functions, post-processings and rotations of batches take an
  execution policy (queue), as the integration. The functions rotating
  batches are loaded by typed getters, and the suffixes of finite strain
  rotations are written once.
- The material state managers know the location of their arrays: the
  functions of the host (update, revert, updateValues, extraction,
  save, restore, integration point by point) refuse arrays located on a
  device. The arrays not given by the initializers may be allocated by a
  memory resource (std::pmr). Memory resources for CUDA and HIP managed
  memory, SYCL and Kokkos shared memory are provided as headers.
- A failure of a batch gives the absolute index of the first failed
  point, and an unexpected status raises an exception.
- The libraries of the stlpar backend stay loaded until the end of the
  process (TBB threads at exit), instead of all the libraries. The sources
  of the batches of the tests are generated again when MFront changes, and
  the native CUDA architecture requires CMake 3.24.
